WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:06.880
Welcome to this OCP Academy course on Open Rack and

2
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:10.540
ORv3. In this first lesson, we'll look at how the

3
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:14.060
Open Compute Project began and how it shaped the Open

4
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:17.120
Rack standard that so many modern data centers rely on

5
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:21.840
today. The Open Compute Project, or OCP, was founded in

6
00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.880
2011 by Facebook, now Meta, together with Intel and Rackspace.

7
00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:31.840
Their goal was to share open source hardware designs that

8
00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:35.580
could make data centers more efficient, scalable, and sustainable.

9
00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:39.080
The origins of Open Rack go back to Facebook's need

10
00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:41.320
to scale its infrastructure efficiently.

11
00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:46.540
Traditional data center designs were full of inefficiencies that added

12
00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:52.060
cost and complexity. The first Open Rack standard, called ORv1,

13
00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.840
launched in 2012 to 2013.

14
00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:59.180
Its centralized power distribution with a 12v DC power shelf

15
00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:03.740
and busbars. This reduced the number of server power cables

16
00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:06.020
and made racks easier to service.

17
00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:11.280
After ORv1, the community developed ORv2.

18
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:15.300
It improved power and rack designs, but was mainly focused

19
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:19.620
on hyperscale data centers, using 21 -inch servers and direct

20
00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:24.600
current power. This made it less common for wider enterprise

21
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:28.320
use, which followed the traditional American Electronic Industries

22
00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.000
Alliance, EIA, standardized 19 -inch rack.

23
00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:37.720
By 2016, the community knew that new challenges like artificial

24
00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:42.100
intelligence and high -performance computing would require even higher

25
00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.300
power densities and better cooling.

26
00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:47.740
So development of ORv3 began.

27
00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:53.980
From 2016 to 2018, companies like Meta, Google, and Microsoft

28
00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:57.700
worked together to identify the need for higher power density,

29
00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:02.300
better cooling solutions, and more flexibility for both hyperscale

30
00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:03.800
and enterprise data centers.

31
00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:10.540
By 2019 and 2020, contributors like Advanced Energy were developing

32
00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:15.600
ORv3 power shelves that used a 48v DC architecture.

33
00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:20.440
This increased efficiency and supported more power -hungry workloads.

34
00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:27.600
In 2021, the OCP Global Summit showcased ORv3's

35
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.600
readiness for broader adoption.

36
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:36.100
Power shelves demonstrated 97 .5 % efficiency and up to

37
00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:39.060
36kW in a 2OU form factor.

38
00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:44.300
By 2022, ORv3 was ready for full -scale deployment.

39
00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:48.060
ORv3 was designed to be flexible.

40
00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:52.720
It supports both 21 -inch open -compute equipment and older,

41
00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.500
standard 19 -inch equipment.

42
00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:59.840
It also includes features like liquid cooling manifolds and blindmate

43
00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:02.260
power connections to make installation easier.

44
00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:07.840
By 2024, adoption of ORv3 had grown significantly.

45
00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:13.680
Companies like Cheval, Eaton, Flex, Rittal, and Sanmina

46
00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.820
have their racks in the OCP marketplace based on the

47
00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:22.200
ORv3 specification. You'll even find racks from wooden data centers

48
00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:24.680
made from low -embedded carbon materials.

49
00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:31.500
In 2025, companies like Meta and NVIDIA contributed advanced designs

50
00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:35.780
such as the Catalina, an MGX AI rack, and Compute

51
00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:39.320
Shelf, which can handle up to 140kW per rack for

52
00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:45.280
powerful AI workloads. ORv3 has changed the data center industry

53
00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:49.680
by improving power efficiency, boosting cooling capabilities, and

54
00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.440
creating a more flexible open ecosystem.

55
00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:58.920
Today, the OCP continues to drive collaboration and standard specifications.

56
00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:03.020
Its open -source hardware approach helps data centers around the

57
00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:06.960
world scale for the future while staying efficient and sustainable

58
00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:11.440
with ever -increasing demands of higher performance computing to power

59
00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:15.820
artificial intelligence that, in turn, will boost the human intelligence

60
00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:17.060
behind every industry.

